    <item>
      <headword id="d0e347">stiodhaidh</headword>
      <description>a steep path down the cliffs to the shore below – the only access to the shore.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="d0e356">maorach</headword>
      <description>this in Bayble, Lewis meant limpets only. These were the commonest bait for the small line.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="d0e365">a’ buain maoraich</headword>
      <description>knocking the limpets off the rocks.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="d0e374">òrd maoraich</headword>
      <description>the short strong knife used for the above [knocking limpets off the rocks] – usually a worn down shortened kitchen knife.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="d0e383">slaopadh a’ mhaoraich</headword>
      <description>covering [limpets] with water and heating sufficiently – and only just – to loosen the shells.</description>
    </item>
